/-
  BSD/Ledger.lean

  Every `sorry` in this tree, enumerated as structured data.

  UNCHECKED.  Not compiled; see BSD/Basic.lean and README.md.

  PURPOSE.  Chapter 51 defines a proof obligation as a tuple (identifier,
  closed statement, status, dependency set, provenance, discharge condition)
  and a ledger as a finite set of such tuples with distinct identifiers and
  dependencies inside the set.  This file is the formal tree's own ledger, in a
  form that Appendix F and the Part VII software can consume mechanically.

  THE INVARIANT THIS FILE EXISTS TO SUPPORT.  Every `sorry` in this tree
  carries a comment `-- OBLIGATION: <id>` on the line immediately above it, and
  every such `<id>` appears exactly once as the `id` field of an entry below.
  Nothing else in the tree may contain `sorry`.  The check is mechanical and is
  performed by `tools/check_tree.py`; its output is reproduced in README.md.

  These identifiers are *local to the formal tree*.  Where an entry corresponds
  to one of the book's proof obligations, the book's identifier is recorded in
  `bookRef`; where it does not, `bookRef` is `none` and that is itself
  information.
-/

import BSD.Basic

namespace BSD

/--
A proof obligation, in the sense of Chapter 51, restricted to what a Lean file
can carry.

Chapter 51's `provenance` and `discharge condition` fields are not modelled:
provenance would have to name a person and a date, and a discharge condition
would have to be machine-checkable, and neither is available here.  Their
absence is a real shortfall against the book's definition and is recorded as
such in README.md.
-/
structure Obligation where
  /-- Unique identifier, matching the `-- OBLIGATION:` comment above the
  `sorry` it stands for.  Never reused. -/
  id : String
  /-- The statement, in one closed English sentence: all quantifiers bound,
  all parameters named, no appeal to context (Chapter 51, requirement (ii)). -/
  statement : String
  /-- The epistemic tag the book attaches to the statement. -/
  tag : RigourTag
  /-- The chapter of the book in which the statement is made. -/
  chapter : Nat
  /-- The module of this tree in which the `sorry` occurs. -/
  moduleName : String
  /-- Other obligations of this ledger that a proof would rely on
  (Chapter 51, requirement (iv): the dependencies of the recorded
  justification, not of the truth of the statement). -/
  dependsOn : List String
  /-- The book's own proof-obligation number, where there is one. -/
  bookRef : Option String
  deriving Repr

/--
The ledger.  Ten entries; ten `sorry`s; one per entry.

Read the `tag` column before anything else.  Three entries are `new` --
elementary statements the book proves informally, which a competent Lean user
could discharge given time.  Four are `known` in the sense that the
mathematics exists in the literature but the Lean prerequisites do not.  Three
are `conditional`: they are the framework-level claims, and each is exactly as
strong as hypotheses that are themselves open.  **No entry is unconditional and
no entry is discharged.**
-/
def ledger : List Obligation :=
  [ { id := "obl-ar-antisymm"
      statement :=
        "For all vertices x and y of the arithmetic fracture diagram, if "
        ++ "x <= y and y <= x then x = y; that is, the relation generated by "
        ++ "the five displayed relations of Chapter 7 is a partial order and "
        ++ "not merely a preorder."
      tag := RigourTag.new
      chapter := 7
      moduleName := "BSD/FractureDiagram.lean"
      dependsOn := []
      bookRef := none },

    { id := "obl-ar-eta-not-prism"
      statement :=
        "For every natural number p, the generic vertex eta is not below the "
        ++ "prismatic vertex at p in the arithmetic fracture diagram: the "
        ++ "generic fibre carries no integral p-adic structure, and the "
        ++ "comparison is imposed at the Fargues-Fontaine vertex instead."
      tag := RigourTag.new
      chapter := 7
      moduleName := "BSD/FractureDiagram.lean"
      dependsOn := []
      bookRef := none },

    { id := "obl-fap-coeff-ext"
      statement :=
        "For every FAP space X and all FAP coefficients M and N on X, if M and "
        ++ "N have the same value at every vertex of the fracture diagram then "
        ++ "M = N."
      tag := RigourTag.new
      chapter := 7
      moduleName := "BSD/FAPSpace.lean"
      dependsOn := []
      bookRef := some "0101" },

    { id := "obl-cont-datum-unique"
      statement :=
        "For every sequence of Dirichlet coefficients, any two continuation "
        ++ "data for it are equal as functions on the complex plane."
      tag := RigourTag.new
      chapter := 48
      moduleName := "BSD/Classical.lean"
      dependsOn := []
      bookRef := none },

    { id := "obl-cont-datum-ne-zero"
      statement :=
        "For every Weierstrass model W over the rationals and every "
        ++ "continuation datum D for the Dirichlet coefficients of W, the "
        ++ "function underlying D is not identically zero."
      tag := RigourTag.new
      chapter := 48
      moduleName := "BSD/Classical.lean"
      dependsOn := []
      bookRef := none },

    { id := "obl-analytic-rank-welldef"
      statement :=
        "For every Weierstrass model W over the rationals and all continuation "
        ++ "data D and D' for the Dirichlet coefficients of W, the order of "
        ++ "vanishing at s = 1 of D agrees with that of D'; hence the analytic "
        ++ "rank depends only on W."
      tag := RigourTag.new
      chapter := 48
      moduleName := "BSD/Classical.lean"
      dependsOn := ["obl-cont-datum-unique", "obl-cont-datum-ne-zero"]
      bookRef := none },

    { id := "obl-mw-rank-zero-iff-torsion"
      statement :=
        "For every Weierstrass model W over the rationals, the Mordell-Weil "
        ++ "rank of W is zero if and only if the Mordell-Weil group of W is a "
        ++ "torsion group."
      tag := RigourTag.known
      chapter := 48
      moduleName := "BSD/Classical.lean"
      dependsOn := []
      bookRef := none },

    { id := "obl-selmer-bound-of-hes"
      statement :=
        "For every Weierstrass model W over the rationals and all natural "
        ++ "numbers l and r, if a higher Euler system for W at l with r legs "
        ++ "exists and the Tate-Shafarevich group of W is finite, then the "
        ++ "Mordell-Weil rank of W is at most r."
      tag := RigourTag.conditional
      chapter := 30
      moduleName := "BSD/Classical.lean"
      dependsOn := []
      bookRef := some "0406" },

    { id := "obl-rank-formula"
      statement :=
        "For every FAP space X satisfying axioms F1-F12, if Conjecture G, the "
        ++ "categorified trace formula, Conjecture A, Conjecture L, Conjecture "
        ++ "M and finiteness of the Tate-Shafarevich group all hold for X, "
        ++ "then the order of vanishing of the FAP zeta function of X at s = 1 "
        ++ "equals the Mordell-Weil rank attached to X."
      tag := RigourTag.conditional
      chapter := 38
      moduleName := "BSD/RankTheorem.lean"
      dependsOn := ["obl-analytic-rank-welldef"]
      bookRef := some "0605" },

    { id := "obl-cert-rank-lower-bound"
      statement :=
        "For every C4 rank-lower-bound certificate c, if the checker accepts c "
        ++ "then the claimed rank of c is at most the Mordell-Weil rank of the "
        ++ "curve named by c."
      tag := RigourTag.conditional
      chapter := 47
      moduleName := "BSD/Certificates.lean"
      dependsOn := ["obl-mw-rank-zero-iff-torsion"]
      bookRef := some "0702" } ]

/-- The number of obligations, and therefore the number of `sorry`s in the
tree.  Checkable by evaluation. -/
theorem ledger_length : ledger.length = 10 := rfl

/-- The identifiers, for cross-checking against the `-- OBLIGATION:` comments
by `tools/check_tree.py`. -/
def ledgerIds : List String := ledger.map Obligation.id

theorem ledgerIds_length : ledgerIds.length = 10 := rfl

/-- The modules that contain a `sorry`. -/
def ledgerModules : List String := ledger.map Obligation.moduleName

/--
The combined epistemic tag of the whole tree: the meet of the tags of all its
obligations, `known` being the unit of the meet.

The value is `conditional`, not `known` and not `new`: the tree as a whole
asserts nothing unconditional.  It is not `open_` only because none of the ten
obligations is itself an open conjecture -- the open conjectures live in
`BSD/Conjectures.lean` as `Prop`s and are never asserted.
-/
def ledgerTag : RigourTag :=
  ledger.foldl (fun t o => RigourTag.meet t o.tag) RigourTag.known

theorem ledgerTag_eq : ledgerTag = RigourTag.conditional := rfl

/--
What the ledger does **not** contain, stated so that the omission cannot be
mistaken for completeness:

* the `axiom` declarations of `BSD/FAPSpace.lean`, `BSD/Axioms.lean`,
  `BSD/Conjectures.lean` and `BSD/Classical.lean`.  These are a larger and more
  dangerous enlargement of the trusted base than the ten `sorry`s, because a
  `sorry` is loud and an `axiom` is quiet.  Chapter 48's axiom-accounting
  proposition is precisely about this: a build whose headline theorem depends
  on an undeclared axiom is worse than no build.
* the twelve axioms F1--F12, which are hypotheses and not obligations.
* the twelve conjectures A--M, which are `Prop`s and are never asserted.
* the fourteen obstructions, which are metadata.

A real continuous-integration rule would compute the transitive axiom set of
every headline declaration and compare it against a published whitelist.  We
cannot run it; the list below is what such a rule would have to whitelist.
-/
def declaredAxiomGroups : List String :=
  [ "BSD/FAPSpace.lean: GeomAt, Comparison, Coherent, CyclotomicAction, "
      ++ "FlowAction, CoeffAt, Glued",
    "BSD/Axioms.lean: the twelve statement predicates F1-F12",
    "BSD/Conjectures.lean: the framework invariants and clause predicates",
    "BSD/Classical.lean: Mordell-Weil, Selmer, Sha, heights, periods, "
      ++ "L-series, modularity, Gross-Zagier-Kolyvagin" ]

theorem declaredAxiomGroups_length : declaredAxiomGroups.length = 4 := rfl

end BSD
